In Redis a "keys containing X" lookup is a **pattern match**, so the reactive way is `SCAN MATCH *<nationalCode>*` (never `KEYS` — it blocks the whole Redis server) and then fetch the values in batches with `MGET`. Below is the implementation, the pipeline/SPI changes it implies, and — importantly — a note on why you'll want an index for this at production throughput.

---

## 1. Updated port: return key/value pairs, not a boolean

```java
public record RedisEntry(String key, String value) {}

public interface NationalCodeLookup {
    /** All Redis entries whose key contains the national code. Empty flux = no match. */
    Flux<RedisEntry> findAll(String nationalCode);
}
```

## 2. `RedisNationalCodeLookup` with SCAN + batched MGET

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class RedisNationalCodeLookup implements NationalCodeLookup {

    private final ReactiveStringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final AppProperties props;

    @Override
    public Flux<RedisEntry> findAll(String nationalCode) {
        String pattern = props.redis().keyPattern().replace("{nc}", escapeGlob(nationalCode)); // e.g. "*{nc}*"

        ScanOptions options = ScanOptions.scanOptions()
                .match(pattern)
                .count(props.redis().scanCount())      // hint per SCAN round-trip, e.g. 1000
                .build();

        return redis.scan(options)                                   // Flux<String> of matching keys, cursor handled for you
                .buffer(props.redis().mgetBatchSize())               // e.g. 100 keys per MGET
                .concatMap(keys -> redis.opsForValue().multiGet(keys)
                        .flatMapMany(values -> Flux.range(0, keys.size())
                                .filter(i -> values.get(i) != null)  // key expired/deleted between SCAN and MGET
                                .map(i -> new RedisEntry(keys.get(i), values.get(i)))))
                .timeout(props.redis().timeout())
                .doOnError(e -> log.warn("Redis lookup failed for nationalCode={}", nationalCode, e));
    }

    /** nationalCode is normally digits, but never let user data inject glob metacharacters. */
    private static String escapeGlob(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(s.length() + 4);
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '*' || c == '?' || c == '[' || c == ']' || c == '\\') sb.append('\\');
            sb.append(c);
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

Config additions:

```java
public record Redis(@NotBlank String keyPattern,   // "*{nc}*"  or narrower e.g. "customer:*:{nc}:*"
                    @Min(10) int scanCount,
                    @Min(1) int mgetBatchSize,
                    Duration timeout) {}
```

```yaml
app:
  redis:
    key-pattern: "*{nc}*"
    scan-count: 1000
    mget-batch-size: 100
    timeout: 2s          # SCAN over a big keyspace takes longer than a single GET
```

### If the matching keys are not all plain strings

`MGET` only works for string values. If some keys hold hashes/sets/lists, dispatch on type instead:

```java
private Mono<RedisEntry> read(String key) {
    return redis.type(key).flatMap(type -> switch (type) {
        case STRING -> redis.opsForValue().get(key)
                            .map(v -> new RedisEntry(key, v));
        case HASH   -> redis.opsForHash().entries(key).collectMap(e -> (String) e.getKey(), e -> (String) e.getValue())
                            .map(m -> new RedisEntry(key, toJson(m)));
        case SET    -> redis.opsForSet().members(key).collectList()
                            .map(l -> new RedisEntry(key, toJson(l)));
        case LIST   -> redis.opsForList().range(key, 0, -1).collectList()
                            .map(l -> new RedisEntry(key, toJson(l)));
        default     -> Mono.empty();
    });
}

// then: redis.scan(options).flatMap(this::read, props.redis().mgetBatchSize())
```

(Slower — one `TYPE` + one read per key — so only use this variant if you truly have mixed types.)

