## 4. ⚠️ Performance: SCAN is a full keyspace walk

`SCAN MATCH *X*` does **not** use an index; Redis iterates the whole keyspace and filters. Cost per message ≈ `totalKeys / scanCount` round-trips. With 10 M keys and `count=1000` that's ~10 000 round-trips **per Kafka message**, which will not keep up with any real topic.

How to make it O(1) while keeping the same `NationalCodeLookup` contract:

### Option A — Narrow the pattern (cheap win, still O(N))
If keys follow a convention like `customer:{nc}:profile`, `loan:{nc}:limit`, use `key-pattern: "*:{nc}:*"` — fewer false matches but SCAN still scans everything. Only OK for small keyspaces (< ~100 k keys) or low message rates.

### Option B — Direct MGET on known key templates (O(1), no SCAN)
If the set of key shapes is finite, list them and skip SCAN entirely:

```yaml
app.redis.key-templates:
  - "customer:{nc}"
  - "loan:{nc}:limit"
  - "blacklist:{nc}"
```

```java
public Flux<RedisEntry> findAll(String nc) {
    List<String> keys = props.redis().keyTemplates().stream().map(t -> t.replace("{nc}", nc)).toList();
    return redis.opsForValue().multiGet(keys)
        .flatMapMany(vals -> Flux.range(0, keys.size())
            .filter(i -> vals.get(i) != null)
            .map(i -> new RedisEntry(keys.get(i), vals.get(i))));
}
```

### Option C — Secondary index SET (O(1), truly "any key containing nc")
Whoever writes the data also does `SADD idx:nc:{nc} <actualKey>`. Lookup becomes:

```java
public Flux<RedisEntry> findAll(String nc) {
    return redis.opsForSet().members("idx:nc:" + nc)
        .collectList()
        .filter(keys -> !keys.isEmpty())
        .flatMapMany(keys -> redis.opsForValue().multiGet(keys)
            .flatMapMany(vals -> Flux.range(0, keys.size())
                .filter(i -> vals.get(i) != null)
                .map(i -> new RedisEntry(keys.get(i), vals.get(i)))));
}
```

If you don't control the writer, run a one-off backfill job (SCAN the whole keyspace once, `SADD` into the index) and keep it current with Redis keyspace notifications (`notify-keyspace-events "KEA"` → subscribe to `__keyevent@0__:set` / `:del` via `ReactiveRedisMessageListenerContainer`).

### Option D — Redis Stack / RediSearch
`FT.SEARCH` on a TAG field for the national code gives indexed lookups with no custom index maintenance — worth it if you're already on Redis Stack.

---

## 5. Recommendation

Keep the **SCAN implementation as a fallback / dev profile** (it's the only thing that literally satisfies "all keys containing the code" against an unknown keyspace), and plug in **Option B or C** as the primary implementation behind the same `NationalCodeLookup` interface — nothing in the pipeline or handlers changes when you swap them:

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.redis.lookup-strategy", havingValue = "scan")
NationalCodeLookup scanLookup(...) { return new RedisNationalCodeLookup(...); }

@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.redis.lookup-strategy", havingValue = "index", matchIfMissing = true)
NationalCodeLookup indexLookup(...) { return new IndexedNationalCodeLookup(...); }
```

One more thing: for Redis **Cluster**, `ReactiveStringRedisTemplate.scan` with Lettuce fans out across master nodes automatically, but `MGET` requires all keys in one hash slot — replace the `multiGet` with `Flux.fromIterable(keys).flatMap(k -> redis.opsForValue().get(k).map(v -> new RedisEntry(k, v)), 32)` in that environment.

